"""
Human Resource Management Question Bank: exactly 34 Easy, 33 Medium, 33 Hard = 100 questions.
Focus: Recruitment, Training, Performance Management, Compensation, Labor Laws, Motivation, Employee Relations, and HR Analytics.
"""

hrm_easy = [
    ("What is the primary distinction between a Job Description and a Job Specification?", "Job Description details duties and responsibilities; Job Specification details qualifications and skills required", ["Job Description sets the monthly salary; Job Specification sets the office location", "Job Description is for contractors; Job Specification is for permanent staff", "Job Description is created by the employee; Job Specification by the client"], "A Job Description defines the role profile and tasks, whereas a Job Specification defines human traits, education, and competencies needed."),
    ("What does 'Human Resource Planning' (HRP) primarily aim to achieve?", "Ensuring the organization has the right number of qualified people in the right jobs at the right time", ["Eliminating all external vendor hiring permanently", "Automating payroll deductions to zero", "Conducting annual union strikes against management"], "Workforce planning aligns current organizational capabilities and future business demands to forecast talent deficits or surpluses."),
    ("In recruitment, what is an 'Internal Source' of candidates?", "Promotions, internal job postings (IJP), and lateral department transfers", ["Campus recruitment drives at regional universities", "Hiring through executive recruitment agencies", "Posting newspaper print advertisements"], "Internal recruitment leverages the existing talent pool, offering career mobility, faster onboarding, and lower hiring costs."),
    ("What is an 'External Source' of recruitment?", "Recruiting talent from outside the enterprise (e.g., job portals, campus hiring)", ["Promoting an existing senior associate to team lead", "Recalling an employee from maternity leave", "Transferring a manager from Delhi branch to Mumbai branch"], "External hiring injects fresh perspectives, specialized technical skills, and industry practices into the organization."),
    ("What does 'Onboarding' or 'Induction' refer to in employee management?", "The systematic process of integrating new hires into the organization's culture, policies, and role expectations", ["The formal exit interview conducted upon resignation", "The disciplinary hearing preceding employee termination", "The annual corporate salary increment review"], "Effective onboarding accelerates time-to-productivity, lowers early turnover, and builds initial psychological engagement."),
    ("What does the 'ADDIE' model stand for in instructional design and employee training?", "Analysis, Design, Development, Implementation, and Evaluation", ["Assessment, Delegation, Delivery, Inspection, and Exit", "Acquisition, Discipline, Direction, Incentives, and Equity", "Action, Documentation, Diagnosis, Integration, and Examination"], "ADDIE is the standard five-phase instructional framework used by HR and L&D teams to create effective training programs."),
    ("In Kirkpatrick's four-level training evaluation model, what does Level 1 evaluate?", "Reaction (how learners felt about and perceived the training experience)", ["Learning (the increase in knowledge or capability)", "Behavior (the extent of applied learning on the job)", "Results (the business impact and financial ROI)"], "Level 1 measures participant satisfaction and perceived relevance through post-training feedback surveys."),
    ("In Kirkpatrick's model, what does Level 2 evaluate?", "Learning (the acquisition of knowledge, skills, and attitudes)", ["Reaction", "Behavior", "Return on Investment"], "Level 2 tests whether participants actually mastered the concepts, often via pre- and post-training assessments."),
    ("In Kirkpatrick's model, what does Level 3 evaluate?", "Behavior (the degree to which participants apply learned skills in their daily work)", ["Reaction", "Learning", "Corporate brand equity"], "Level 3 evaluates workplace behavioral transfer and operational adoption of competencies over 3 to 6 months."),
    ("In Kirkpatrick's model, what does Level 4 evaluate?", "Results (the targeted organizational outcomes such as productivity, sales, and error reduction)", ["Reaction", "Course attendance percentages", "Classroom snack ratings"], "Level 4 assesses high-level business impact, comparing training outcomes against organizational bottom-line metrics."),
    ("What does a '360-Degree Feedback' appraisal system incorporate?", "Performance evaluations from superiors, peers, subordinates, and self-assessment (and sometimes customers)", ["An assessment conducted solely by the immediate direct manager", "An automated algorithm tracking keystroke counts on keyboards", "A quarterly inspection by the ministry of labor"], "360-degree appraisal provides a balanced, holistic evaluation of leadership, collaboration, and work behaviors."),
    ("What does 'MBO' stand for in performance management?", "Management by Objectives", ["Management by Observation", "Monitoring Business Output", "Measurement of Baseline Operations"], "Pioneered by Peter Drucker, MBO aligns individual employee goals with overarching corporate strategic milestones."),
    ("In performance management, what does 'BARS' stand for?", "Behaviorally Anchored Rating Scales", ["Baseline Assessment of Realized Skills", "Business Action and Revenue Standards", "Behavioral Audit for Retention Strategies"], "BARS combines qualitative critical incidents with quantitative ratings, providing concrete behavioral anchors across rating levels."),
    ("What is the 'Halo Effect' in performance appraisal rating errors?", "Allowing one outstanding positive characteristic of an employee to bias the overall appraisal favorably", ["Rating all employees strictly as average to avoid conflict", "Rating recent performance while ignoring earlier months of work", "Judging an employee based on their university degree alone"], "The halo error occurs when a supervisor generalizes one strong trait (e.g., punctuality) to conclude the person excels at everything."),
    ("What is the 'Horns Effect' in performance appraisal?", "Allowing one perceived negative trait or weakness to disproportionately taint the entire evaluation", ["Giving high ratings only to employees who share personal hobbies", "Grading employees higher than they deserve out of sympathy", "Assuming all junior staff will fail without supervision"], "The horns effect is the inverse of the halo effect: a single flaw colors the appraiser's judgment negatively across all criteria."),
    ("What is the 'Recency Bias' in employee performance appraisals?", "Overemphasizing an employee's most recent actions rather than evaluating performance across the full year", ["Giving equal ratings to every team member", "Favoring candidates who graduated from elite colleges", "Relying exclusively on self-evaluation forms"], "Recency error occurs when managers focus disproportionately on events from the last few weeks before review submission."),
    ("What is 'Central Tendency Bias' in rating employee performance?", "A manager's tendency to rate all employees near the middle of the scale, avoiding high or low marks", ["Rating only employees working in corporate headquarters", "Giving failing grades to the majority of workers", "Ranking team members alphabetically"], "Central tendency error avoids contentious extreme ratings, failing to distinguish top performers from underperformers."),
    ("According to Abraham Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs, what is the most fundamental baseline tier?", "Physiological Needs (food, water, shelter, rest)", ["Safety and Security Needs", "Belonging and Love Needs", "Self-Actualization Needs"], "Maslow's five tiers progress from basic biological survival necessities up to higher psychological and self-fulfillment needs."),
    ("According to Maslow's hierarchy, what sits at the pinnacle (highest level) of human needs?", "Self-Actualization (achieving one's full potential and personal growth)", ["Esteem Needs", "Social Belonging Needs", "Safety Needs"], "Self-actualization represents the desire to maximize individual creative potential and achieve meaningful personal mastery."),
    ("In Frederick Herzberg's Dual-Factor Theory, which factors prevent job dissatisfaction but do NOT motivate?", "Hygiene Factors (salary, company policies, physical working conditions, job security)", ["Motivator Factors (achievement, recognition, responsibility, growth)", "Intrinsic psychological drives", "Self-actualization triggers"], "Herzberg showed that hygiene factors merely prevent discontent; true motivation stems from intrinsic satisfiers (motivators)."),
    ("In Herzberg's theory, which factors genuinely drive job satisfaction, engagement, and superior performance?", "Motivators (recognition, meaningful work, advancement, achievement, personal growth)", ["Salary, paid leaves, and parking spots", "Office air conditioning and furniture", "Company stationery and computer monitors"], "Motivators fulfill higher-order psychological needs, inspiring proactive discretionary effort and commitment."),
    ("In Douglas McGregor's Theory X and Theory Y, what does a 'Theory X' manager assume about employees?", "Employees naturally dislike work, avoid responsibility, and must be closely controlled and coerced", ["Employees are intrinsically motivated and enjoy creative problem-solving", "Employees seek self-direction and thrive under autonomy", "Employees view work as natural as play or rest"], "Theory X reflects an authoritarian management style emphasizing micromanagement, strict supervision, and disciplinary penalties."),
    ("In Douglas McGregor's framework, what does a 'Theory Y' manager assume about employees?", "Employees are self-directed, seek responsibility, and find work inherently natural and fulfilling", ["Employees are inherently lazy and motivated only by fear of firing", "Employees prefer routine orders and hate making decisions", "Employees have zero ambition and avoid working whenever possible"], "Theory Y reflects participative leadership that empowers employees through trust, delegation, and continuous development."),
    ("What does the 'POSH Act' in India mandate for workplaces with 10 or more employees?", "Constitution of an Internal Complaints Committee (ICC) to address sexual harassment grievances", ["A mandatory 40-hour workweek cap for all executives", "Free daily catered lunches for all staff members", "An annual bonus equal to three months of gross salary"], "The Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act, 2013 mandates an ICC in every workplace."),
    ("Under the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972, what is the minimum continuous service required for gratuity eligibility?", "5 years of continuous service (except in cases of death or disablement)", ["1 year of service", "3 years of service", "10 years of service"], "An employee who renders at least 5 years of continuous service with an establishment is statutorily entitled to gratuity upon separation."),
    ("Under the Employees' Provident Funds and Miscellaneous Provisions Act, 1952, what is the standard employee contribution rate?", "12% of basic wages, dearness allowance, and retaining allowance", ["8.33% of gross salary", "5% of net salary", "20% of basic pay"], "The employee contributes 12% of basic pay and DA, matched by an equal 12% employer contribution (split into EPF and EPS)."),
    ("What does 'Attrition Rate' measure in human resource analytics?", "The percentage of employees who leave an organization over a specific time period (voluntary or involuntary)", ["The speed at which new employees are promoted to management", "The percentage of candidates who accept initial job offers", "The number of hours employees spend in annual training"], "Attrition Rate = (Number of Leavers / Average Number of Employees) * 100, tracking organizational talent retention health."),
    ("What is an 'Exit Interview'?", "A structured survey or conversation conducted with a departing employee to understand reasons for leaving", ["A technical interview conducted before final job offer", "A disciplinary review before imposing salary deductions", "A celebration dinner held on an employee's work anniversary"], "Exit interviews provide unfiltered feedback on management practices, workplace culture, compensation, and retention risks."),
    ("What does 'Cost-per-Hire' (CPH) evaluate in talent acquisition?", "The total financial investment incurred to attract, recruit, and onboard a new hire", ["The gross annual salary paid to a newly recruited candidate", "The cost of building a dedicated office cubicle for staff", "The fee charged by employment verification government agencies"], "CPH aggregates internal recruiting costs and external agency/advertising expenses divided by the total number of hires."),
    ("What does 'Time-to-Fill' measure in recruitment operations?", "The total number of calendar days between job requisition approval and the candidate's offer acceptance", ["The time taken by an employee to drive to work", "The duration of the final interview panel meeting", "The length of the probationary training program"], "Time-to-fill monitors talent acquisition operational efficiency and capacity to meet business hiring demands promptly."),
    ("What does 'ESOP' stand for in corporate compensation plans?", "Employee Stock Ownership Plan", ["Executive Salary Overtime Package", "Employment Standard Operating Procedure", "Equal Share Organization Protocol"], "ESOPs grant employees rights to purchase corporate equity shares at a predetermined strike price, aligning long-term interests."),
    ("What is 'Job Enlargement' (horizontal job loading)?", "Adding more tasks of similar complexity to an employee's existing job to reduce monotony", ["Promoting an employee to executive board director", "Increasing employee basic salary by 50%", "Sending an employee on an overseas international transfer"], "Job enlargement broadens the scope of activities at the same hierarchical tier to maintain interest and operational versatility."),
    ("What is 'Job Enrichment' (vertical job loading)?", "Redesigning a job to incorporate greater autonomy, decision-making responsibility, and growth opportunities", ["Increasing the number of repetitive assembly-line tasks", "Extending mandatory overtime work hours on weekends", "Providing free transportation and company vehicles"], "Job enrichment empowers workers with control over task planning and execution, boosting intrinsic motivation."),
    ("What is 'Moonlighting' in contemporary human resource policy?", "Taking up secondary employment or side gigs while maintaining full-time primary employment without employer consent", ["Working exclusive night shifts at a 24-hour call center", "Working remotely from vacation resorts", "Volunteering for charitable causes on weekends"], "Moonlighting raises concerns regarding conflict of interest, intellectual property leakage, fatigue, and employer policy breaches.")
]

hrm_med = [
    ("What are the three core needs identified in David McClelland's Acquired Needs Theory?", "Need for Achievement (nAch), Need for Affiliation (nAff), and Need for Power (nPow)", ["Need for Food, Need for Shelter, and Need for Water", "Need for Salary, Need for Status, and Need for Security", "Need for Autonomy, Need for Competence, and Need for Relatedness"], "McClelland posited that individuals are driven by varying proportions of achievement mastery, affiliation belonging, and power influence."),
    ("In Victor Vroom's Expectancy Theory of Motivation, what does 'Expectancy' represent?", "The subjective belief that one's effort will result in successful task performance (Effort -> Performance)", ["The belief that successful performance will lead to desired rewards", "The personal value or attractiveness placed on the expected reward", "The fear of disciplinary punishment for missing targets"], "Vroom's formula is Motivation = Expectancy * Instrumentality * Valence; if any component equals zero, motivation collapses."),
    ("In Vroom's Expectancy Theory, what does 'Instrumentality' represent?", "The belief that achieving the performance standard will lead to the promised outcome or reward (Performance -> Reward)", ["The trust in organizational leadership integrity", "The technical proficiency required to use office equipment", "The emotional connection felt toward fellow team members"], "Instrumentality measures the perceived reliability of the reward system: will management actually deliver the bonus if targets are met?"),
    ("In Vroom's Expectancy Theory, what does 'Valence' represent?", "The positive or negative value and importance an employee places on the anticipated reward or outcome", ["The amount of physical exertion expended during the task", "The number of hours worked per calendar week", "The percentage of company equity owned by the worker"], "Valence reflects personal preferences (e.g., one employee may value cash bonuses, while another prioritizes flexible hours)."),
    ("In J. Stacy Adams' Equity Theory, how do employees evaluate workplace fairness?", "By comparing their ratio of outcomes (rewards) to inputs (efforts) with the outcome/input ratio of relevant referent others", ["By comparing their current pay with their highest childhood dream", "By calculating the exact gross revenue of the enterprise", "By checking whether their manager has a university degree"], "Perceived inequity (under-reward or over-reward) creates psychological distress, driving behaviors to restore cognitive balance."),
    ("In the 9-Box Grid talent assessment matrix, what two dimensions are plotted on the X and Y axes?", "Performance (historical delivery) and Potential (future growth capacity)", ["Salary and Years of Tenure", "Technical Competence and IQ Score", "Attendance and Customer Feedback"], "The 9-box grid categorizes employees (e.g., High Potential/High Performance 'Stars') to plan succession and retention actions."),
    ("What is 'Succession Planning' in strategic human resource management?", "The systematic process of identifying and developing high-potential internal candidates to fill key leadership roles", ["The immediate hiring of external agency contractors when a manager quits", "The legal division of company assets upon corporate liquidation", "The calculation of annual retirement pensions for union laborers"], "Succession planning ensures leadership continuity, institutional stability, and prevents disruption when critical executives depart."),
    ("In compensation management, what does 'Compa-Ratio' (Comparative Ratio) measure?", "The relationship between an employee's actual base salary and the midpoint of the salary range for that job grade", ["The ratio of male employee salaries to female employee salaries", "The percentage of salary paid out as cash year-end bonus", "The proportion of total company payroll to annual revenue"], "Compa-Ratio = (Actual Salary / Salary Range Midpoint) * 100; a ratio of 100% means the employee is paid exactly at market midpoint."),
    ("What is 'Broadbanding' in corporate compensation structure design?", "Consolidating numerous narrow salary pay grades into a few wide pay bands with large salary ranges", ["Eliminating cash salaries in favor of 100% stock options", "Paying all employees in the enterprise the identical flat rate", "Publishing everyone's monthly paychecks on public notice boards"], "Broadbanding flattens job hierarchies, facilitates lateral transfers, and reduces bureaucratic grade-reclassification requests."),
    ("In compensation management, what is 'Internal Equity'?", "Ensuring that jobs of higher relative value within the organization receive commensurately higher pay", ["Paying higher wages than competing external market firms", "Distributing identical bonuses regardless of individual performance", "Paying overseas remote workers in foreign currencies"], "Internal equity is established through systematic Job Evaluation (e.g., Point-Factor method) to establish fair internal wage hierarchies."),
    ("In compensation management, what is 'External Equity'?", "Ensuring organizational pay levels are competitive with prevailing wages paid by other employers in the industry", ["Matching executive pay to employee trade union demands", "Equalizing wages across all global regional subsidiaries", "Paying contractors more than permanent salaried staff"], "External equity relies on salary benchmarking surveys to ensure the organization can attract and retain market-competitive talent."),
    ("Under the Code on Wages, 2019 in India, wages must comprise what minimum percentage of the total remuneration package?", "At least 50% of the total remuneration (allowances cannot exceed 50%)", ["At least 25%", "At least 70%", "At least 90%"], "The 50% rule prevents employers from artificially depressing basic pay to minimize statutory liabilities for PF and gratuity."),
    ("What is the primary objective of the 'Standing Orders' under the Industrial Employment (Standing Orders) Act, 1946?", "Formally defining conditions of employment, disciplinary rules, attendance, and misconduct procedures for workmen", ["Fixing the daily retail selling price of manufactured goods", "Regulating the import tariffs on foreign raw materials", "Appointing external audit firms for financial reporting"], "Standing orders provide clear, codified conditions of employment to minimize industrial friction and arbitrary discipline."),
    ("In industrial dispute resolution, what is the role of a 'Conciliation Officer'?", "A government official appointed to mediate negotiations between employers and unions to foster amicable settlements", ["A judge who delivers final criminal sentences to striking workers", "A corporate consultant hired to fire unionized employees", "A labor union president leading industrial strikes"], "Conciliation officers assist parties in reaching mutual bilateral settlements without formal judicial litigation."),
    ("What is 'Voluntary Arbitration' under Section 10A of the Industrial Disputes Act, 1947?", "Parties mutually agree in writing to refer a dispute to an independent arbitrator whose award is binding", ["The government forcibly banning all trade union assemblies", "The management unilaterally reducing worker wage rates", "Workers submitting grievances to local civil police"], "Arbitration offers a consensual, faster alternative to lengthy industrial tribunal adjudication."),
    ("What is a 'Lockout' as defined in industrial relations?", "The temporary closing of a place of employment or suspension of work by an employer during a labor dispute", ["A cessation of work by employees acting in concert", "The permanent liquidation of an unprofitable factory", "A government curfew imposed during public elections"], "A lockout is an employer's collective bargaining weapon, whereas a strike is the employees' collective weapon."),
    ("What is a 'Gherao' in Indian labor relations history?", "A physical blockade where workers encircle managerial personnel to coerce agreement to labor demands", ["A formal written tripartite wage agreement", "An educational scholarship provided to workers' children", "A voluntary resignation submitted by union representatives"], "Courts in India have consistently ruled that coercive, intimidating Gherao actions are illegal and non-peaceful."),
    ("What is the 'Point Method' of job evaluation?", "A quantitative technique where jobs are evaluated and assigned points across compensable factors (skill, effort, responsibility)", ["Ranking jobs intuitively from highest paid to lowest paid", "Classifying jobs based on the color of employee work uniforms", "Setting salaries based on the number of daily phone calls"], "The point-factor method breaks jobs into compensable factors with defined degree levels to calculate relative internal job worth."),
    ("In human resource analytics, what does 'Employee Net Promoter Score' (eNPS) measure?", "Employee loyalty and willingness to recommend the organization as a great place to work", ["The percentage of employees promoted within one calendar year", "The customer satisfaction score generated by retail sales staff", "The average annual revenue generated per employee"], "eNPS asks: 'On a scale of 0-10, how likely are you to recommend this company?' (Promoters % minus Detractors %)."),
    ("What is 'Human Capital ROI' (HCROI) calculated as?", "[Revenue - (Operating Expenses - Total Labor Costs)] / Total Labor Costs", ["Total Revenue / Total Number of Employees", "Net Profit / Total Shareholder Equity", "Total Training Budget / Number of Trained Workers"], "HCROI calculates the return on investment generated for every dollar spent on employee compensation and benefits."),
    ("In talent acquisition, what is a 'Realistic Job Preview' (RJP)?", "Providing candidates with an honest, balanced picture of both positive and challenging aspects of the role", ["Exaggerating job benefits to convince candidates to sign offers", "Showing candidates a glamorous promotional video of corporate retreats", "Hiding difficult working conditions until after the probation period"], "RJPs reduce early voluntary turnover by aligning candidate expectations with actual operational realities."),
    ("What is the 'Glass Ceiling' phenomenon in organizational sociology?", "An invisible, unacknowledged barrier that prevents qualified women and minorities from advancing to executive leadership", ["A transparent glass roof installed in modern office atriums", "A mandatory retirement age enforced by corporate bylaws", "A fixed salary cap imposed by government wage commissions"], "The glass ceiling represents subtle institutional and cultural prejudices hindering demographic diversity at top ranks."),
    ("What is 'Job Sharing' in flexible work arrangements?", "Two part-time employees formally sharing the duties, hours, and pay of one full-time position", ["Two colleagues sitting at the same computer desk simultaneously", "Rotating employees across different corporate departments weekly", "Delegating one's personal work tasks to an unpaid intern"], "Job sharing offers work-life flexibility while preserving continuous full-time operational coverage for the role."),
    ("What is 'Assessment Center' methodology in talent selection and promotion?", "A multi-exercise evaluation where multiple assessors evaluate candidates on standardized behavioral simulations and roleplays", ["A centralized medical diagnostic clinic for pre-employment checks", "A computer examination center for typing speed tests", "An executive boardroom where annual bonuses are decided"], "Assessment centers use in-basket exercises, leaderless group discussions, and case studies to gauge future competencies."),
    ("In performance management, what is 'Forced Distribution' (the Bell Curve model)?", "A system where managers are mandated to allocate employees into predetermined percentage buckets (e.g., 10% top, 70% middle, 20% bottom)", ["Giving every employee an identical 5% salary increment", "Allowing employees to pick their own performance rating", "Rating workers purely on the number of years served"], "Popularized by GE's Jack Welch, forced distribution aims to eliminate rating leniency but often triggers unhealthy internal competition."),
    ("What are 'OKRs' (Objectives and Key Results)?", "A goal-setting framework connecting ambitious qualitative objectives with 3 to 5 measurable quantitative outcomes", ["A disciplinary procedure for terminating underperforming staff", "An accounting calculation for payroll tax withholdings", "An insurance policy covering workplace machinery breakdowns"], "OKRs, popularized by Google, foster transparency, ambitious stretch goals, and rapid quarterly alignment."),
    ("What does the 'Balanced Scorecard' (Kaplan & Norton) incorporate to measure performance?", "Financial, Customer, Internal Business Processes, and Learning & Growth perspectives", ["Only historical financial earnings and balance sheet debt", "Employee attendance records and biometric swipe logs", "Executive stock option grants and quarterly dividend yields"], "The balanced scorecard prevents short-term financial tunnel vision by measuring underlying operational and capability drivers."),
    ("What is 'Cross-Training' in workforce capability building?", "Training employees to perform duties outside their primary role to increase operational agility and redundancy", ["Enrolling employees in competitive athletic sports programs", "Training employees in multiple religious traditions", "Rotating employees between competing external firms"], "Cross-training mitigates single-point dependency risks and allows flexible re-deployment during workload surges."),
    ("What is 'Presenteeism' in workplace occupational health?", "Employees attending work physically while unwell or disengaged, leading to sub-optimal productivity and error rates", ["Arriving at the office exactly 15 minutes before shift start", "Submitting daily timesheets with 100% attendance records", "Winning an award for zero sick leave taken in a year"], "Presenteeism costs organizations more in lost output and contagious illness spread than outright legitimate absenteeism."),
    ("What is 'Quiet Quitting' in contemporary workplace terminology?", "Doing only the bare minimum tasks required by one's job description, setting strict boundaries without going above and beyond", ["Resigning from a job silently without submitting an official letter", "Working secretly for a competitor during office hours", "Stealing confidential company client databases"], "Quiet quitting reflects disengagement where employees refuse unpaid overtime and burnout culture, rejecting 'hustle' ethos."),
    ("In training design, what is 'Blended Learning'?", "An instructional model combining self-paced digital/e-learning with interactive instructor-led classroom sessions", ["Mixing employees from different departments in the same cafeteria", "Combining payroll deductions with voluntary charitable gifts", "Teaching technical coding alongside foreign languages"], "Blended learning maximizes pedagogical effectiveness, leveraging digital flexibility alongside collaborative live coaching."),
    ("What is 'Bona Fide Occupational Qualification' (BFOQ) in employment non-discrimination law?", "A legitimate, necessary qualification that is reasonably essential to the normal operation of a particular business", ["A university degree issued by an accredited institution", "A medical certificate verifying eyesight standards", "A personal character recommendation letter from a local judge"], "BFOQ allows exemptions to discrimination rules in rare instances where a specific demographic characteristic is truly essential (e.g., hiring female guards for women's prisons)."),
    ("Under the Apprentices Act, 1961, what is the core legal obligation of designated establishments?", "Providing practical workplace apprenticeship training in accordance with prescribed syllabi and paying statutory stipends", ["Guaranteeing permanent salaried employment to all apprentices", "Paying full executive bonus packages from day one", "Exempting apprentices from all factory safety protocols"], "The Apprentices Act promotes skill formation, requiring industries to engage a prescribed quota of apprentices with basic stipends.")
]

hrm_hard = [
    ("In the Ulrich HR Model, what are the four distinct strategic roles of HR professionals?", "Strategic Partner, Administrative Expert, Employee Champion, and Change Agent", ["Recruiter, Payroll Clerk, Union Negotiator, and Disciplinary Officer", "Executive Director, Chief Accountant, Operations Manager, and Auditor", "Talent Hunter, Welfare Warden, Compliance Inspector, and Trainer"], "Dave Ulrich's framework aligns HR deliverables with business strategy, operational efficiency, employee needs, and organizational change."),
    ("Under the Code on Industrial Relations, 2020, what is the revised employee threshold for requiring prior government permission for closure or retrenchment?", "300 or more workers (increased from the previous 100 workers under the 1947 Act)", ["500 or more workers", "200 or more workers", "50 or more workers"], "The threshold reform provides greater flexibility to medium-sized manufacturing enterprises to adjust operations without prior state sanction."),
    ("Under the Code on Industrial Relations, 2020, what is the mandatory notice period required before workers can go on strike?", "At least 14 days and not more than 60 days advance written notice for ALL industrial establishments", ["Zero notice (strikes can be called instantly)", "Only 24 hours notice for utility providers", "Notice is required only if the union is unregistered"], "The 2020 Code extended strike notice mandates to all industrial sectors, eliminating sudden flash wildcat strikes."),
    ("Under the Code on Social Security, 2020, how are 'Gig Workers' and 'Platform Workers' formally integrated?", "They are legally recognized with dedicated social security schemes and a National Social Security Fund funded by aggregators", ["They are classified as permanent full-time government civil servants", "They are declared completely exempt from all labor regulations", "They are banned from providing services through digital platforms"], "The 2020 Code brings gig delivery and app-based ride-hailing drivers into statutory welfare frameworks through aggregator contributions."),
    ("In Hackman and Oldham's Job Characteristics Model, what are the five core job dimensions that drive critical psychological states?", "Skill Variety, Task Identity, Task Significance, Autonomy, and Feedback", ["Salary, Benefits, Promotion Speed, Security, and Comfort", "Education, Seniority, Punctuality, Compliance, and Loyalty", "Team Size, Office Space, Travel Allowance, Bonuses, and Tech"], "These five dimensions foster experienced meaningfulness, responsibility, and knowledge of results, boosting work outcomes."),
    ("In the Job Characteristics Model, what is the formula to calculate the Motivating Potential Score (MPS)?", "MPS = [(Skill Variety + Task Identity + Task Significance) / 3] * Autonomy * Feedback", ["MPS = Skill Variety * Task Identity * Task Significance * Autonomy * Feedback", "MPS = (Skill Variety + Autonomy) * (Task Significance + Feedback)", "MPS = (Salary + Benefits) * Promotion Likelihood"], "Autonomy and Feedback act as multiplicative multipliers; if either is zero, overall motivating potential drops to zero."),
    ("In psychometrics and employment testing, what does 'Construct Validity' evaluate?", "The degree to which a test measures the hypothetical construct or psychological trait it purports to measure (e.g., leadership)", ["The extent to which test questions match the exact job description tasks", "The correlation between test scores and actual future job performance ratings", "The visual appeal and layout design of the printed exam sheets"], "Construct validity evaluates whether a test truly captures theoretical underlying competencies like emotional intelligence or spatial logic."),
    ("In employment testing, what does 'Criterion-Related Validity' measure?", "The statistical relationship between test scores and actual job performance criteria (via predictive or concurrent studies)", ["The grammatical accuracy of the examination questions", "The percentage of candidates who finish the exam on time", "The legal licensing fees paid to test publishing companies"], "Criterion validity confirms whether scoring well on the pre-hire assessment statistically predicts superior on-the-job productivity."),
    ("What is the 'Adverse Impact' (Four-Fifths Rule or 80% Rule) in equal employment opportunity compliance?", "Selection rate for a protected group is less than 80% of the selection rate for the group with the highest selection rate", ["80% of employees must receive identical annual pay bonuses", "Women must constitute at least 80% of corporate board directors", "No company can reject more than 20% of job applicants in a year"], "The 80% rule serves as a mathematical screening test to flag systemic, unintended discrimination in pre-employment selection procedures."),
    ("In high-volume recruitment, what does the 'Selection Ratio' represent?", "Total Number of Hired Candidates divided by Total Number of Applicants (Hired / Applicants)", ["Total Number of Hired Candidates divided by Total Vacancies", "Total Recruiting Costs divided by Number of Hires", "Total Interviewers divided by Number of Candidates"], "A low selection ratio (e.g., hiring 2 out of 100 applicants) indicates high hiring selectivity, enhancing the utility of valid test instruments."),
    ("In utility analysis of employee selection, what does the 'Taylor-Russell Tables' estimate?", "The increase in the proportion of successful employees expected from using a valid selection test over random selection", ["The cost savings achieved by outsourcing payroll processing", "The attrition probability of senior executives based on age", "The optimal number of vacation days to minimize sick leaves"], "Taylor-Russell tables quantify selection test utility based on test validity, selection ratio, and base rate of success."),
    ("What does the 'Brodel-Hegarty-Cronbach' utility model calculate for HR investments?", "The net financial dollar return yielded by an employee selection program over baseline random selection", ["The tax deductions allowable for employee corporate retreats", "The commercial real estate value of corporate office spaces", "The legal damages payable in wrongful dismissal lawsuits"], "Utility models quantify HR program economic benefits: Dollar Gain = N * T * r_xy * SD_y * Z_x - Cost of Testing."),
    ("In strategic HRM, what does the 'Resource-Based View' (RBV) postulate regarding competitive advantage?", "Human resources create sustainable competitive advantage if they are Valuable, Rare, Inimitable, and Non-substitutable (VRIN)", ["Competitive advantage stems solely from buying cheaper factory machinery", "Any business model can be copied instantly by hiring external consultants", "Physical financial capital is the only enduring competitive asset"], "Jay Barney's RBV framework demonstrates that unique human capital pools and complex organizational cultures cannot be easily copied."),
    ("In strategic HRM, what is the difference between 'Vertical Fit' and 'Horizontal Fit'?", "Vertical Fit aligns HR practices with corporate business strategy; Horizontal Fit ensures internal coherence among all HR practices", ["Vertical Fit relates to salaries; Horizontal Fit relates to office layouts", "Vertical Fit applies to junior staff; Horizontal Fit applies to executives", "Vertical Fit is domestic compliance; Horizontal Fit is international trade"], "High performance requires both vertical alignment with competitive positioning and horizontal synergy across recruitment, PMS, and rewards."),
    ("What does 'Human Resource Accounting' (HRA) attempt to measure?", "The economic measurement, valuation, and reporting of human capital investments and asset value to the organization", ["The calculation of monthly income tax withholdings from payroll", "The tracking of petty cash disbursements for office lunches", "The auditing of statutory workers' provident fund bank balances"], "HRA (using Lev & Schwartz or Flamholtz models) treats employee talent, training, and capabilities as valuable capital assets rather than pure costs."),
    ("In labor economics, what does the 'Efficiency Wage Theory' explain?", "Employers pay above-market wages because higher pay increases worker productivity, reduces turnover, and attracts top talent", ["Workers will accept below-minimum wages if free snacks are offered", "Wage rates are determined strictly by government administrative decrees", "Productivity decreases automatically as salaries are raised"], "Paying above market market-clearing wages discourages shirking, cuts hiring costs, and fosters reciprocity and morale."),
    ("In labor economics, what does the 'Compensating Wage Differential' account for?", "Higher wages paid to compensate workers for undesirable, hazardous, or dangerous working conditions (e.g., offshore rigs)", ["Bonuses paid to staff who work in luxury corporate headquarters", "Salary deductions imposed when employees take medical leaves", "Higher wages paid to workers who hold theoretical academic degrees"], "Compensating differentials equalize net advantage across occupations, offering hazard pay to attract workers to risky tasks."),
    ("What does 'Human Resource Information System' (HRIS) integration with ERP enable?", "Unified real-time data flows connecting core HR, payroll, talent analytics, enterprise finance, and supply chain", ["Standalone spreadsheets kept on individual managers' laptop computers", "Manual paper ledger filing in locked basement archival rooms", "Restricting all employee data access strictly to external auditors"], "Enterprise HRIS integration unifies workforce planning with financial budgeting, operational capacity, and corporate performance tracking."),
    ("In organizational network analysis (ONA), what is a 'Central Connector'?", "An employee who holds a disproportionate number of informal collaborative relationships and information flows across the company", ["The main computer router located in the corporate data center", "The receptionist stationed at the building entrance lobby", "The chief executive officer on the official organizational chart"], "ONA reveals informal informal influence: central connectors share knowledge widely, but can become communication bottlenecks or single-point burnout risks."),
    ("In Organizational Network Analysis (ONA), what is a 'Broker' or 'Boundary Spanner'?", "An individual who bridges structural holes between otherwise disconnected departments or silos", ["A stock trader managing the company equity investment portfolio", "An external headhunter hired to source executive talent", "An insurance agent selling employee group health cover"], "Boundary spanners facilitate cross-pollination of innovative ideas and inter-departmental collaboration across organizational silos."),
    ("In change management, what are the three stages of Kurt Lewin's Change Model?", "Unfreezing, Changing (Transitioning), and Refreezing", ["Planning, Doing, and Checking", "Initiating, Executing, and Auditing", "Storming, Norming, and Performing"], "Lewin showed that effective change requires breaking current inertia (Unfreezing), moving to new behaviors, and anchoring them into culture (Refreezing)."),
    ("In John Kotter's 8-Step Change Model, what is the critical first step?", "Creating a sense of urgency", ["Forming a strategic vision", "Empowering broad-based action", "Anchoring new approaches in culture"], "Without urgent recognition of current threats or opportunities, complacency kills change initiatives before they start."),
    ("What is 'Job Embeddedness' theory in employee retention?", "The web of organizational and community links, fit, and perceived sacrifice that keeps an employee attached to their current role", ["Embedding microchip tracking devices into employee identity cards", "Forcing employees to sign 10-year non-compete employment bonds", "Restricting employees from interacting with former university classmates"], "Mitchell and Lee showed that off-the-job community roots and organizational mesh prevent turnover even when job satisfaction fluctuates."),
    ("What is 'Organizational Citizenship Behavior' (OCB)?", "Discretionary, voluntary behaviors that are not part of formal job requirements but promote effective organizational functioning", ["Voting in national parliamentary elections during work hours", "Following basic mandatory workplace safety rules without violation", "Filing weekly timesheets prior to the Friday afternoon deadline"], "OCBs (altruism, conscientiousness, sportsmanship, courtesy, civic virtue) lubricate organizational machinery and foster collaborative agility."),
    ("What is 'Counterproductive Work Behavior' (CWB)?", "Intentional employee behaviors that harm the organization, its members, or legitimate business interests (e.g., theft, sabotage, bullying)", ["Making unintentional honest mathematical errors on balance sheets", "Taking authorized medical sick leave when suffering from pneumonia", "Asking challenging technical questions during team meetings"], "CWBs range from interpersonal aggression and gossiping to organizational sabotage, fraud, and deliberate output restriction."),
    ("In emotional intelligence (EQ) theory (Daniel Goleman), what are the five core components?", "Self-Awareness, Self-Regulation, Motivation, Empathy, and Social Skills", ["Intellectual Quotient, Memory, Logic, Calculation, and Speed", "Extroversion, Agreeableness, Neuroticism, Openness, and Conscientiousness", "Power, Wealth, Dominance, Control, and Compliance"], "Goleman demonstrated that emotional competencies correlate more strongly with executive leadership effectiveness than raw cognitive IQ."),
    ("In employee relations, what is the 'Psychological Contract'?", "The unwritten, implicit set of mutual expectations, beliefs, and obligations between an employee and their employer", ["A certified medical psychiatric clearance certificate for new hires", "A legally binding notarized non-disclosure intellectual agreement", "The printed employee code of conduct handbook given on day one"], "Breaches of psychological contract (e.g., broken unwritten promises regarding career growth or fairness) destroy trust and drive rapid attrition."),
    ("In labor economics, what does 'Internal Labor Market' (ILM) refer to?", "An administrative mechanism where entry is restricted to bottom tiers and higher jobs are filled via promotion from within", ["Hiring day laborers from local street corners for cash", "A temporary agency providing short-term secretarial coverage", "The recruitment of foreign expatriate technical specialists"], "Doeringer and Piore demonstrated that ILMs protect enterprise-specific skills, foster loyalty, and insulate wages from open market volatility."),
    ("Under the Indian Trade Unions Act, 1926, what is the minimum number of members required to apply for registration of a trade union?", "At least 7 members, with at least 10% or 100 workers (whichever is less) engaged in the establishment", ["At least 2 members", "At least 50% of the entire national workforce", "At least 500 members unconditionally"], "The statutory threshold ensures representative collective bargaining and prevents mushrooming of frivolous microscopic unions."),
    ("Under the Factories Act, 1948, a 'Welfare Officer' must be mandatorily appointed in every factory employing:", "500 or more workers", ["100 or more workers", "1,000 or more workers", "50 or more workers"], "Section 49 mandates dedicated welfare officers to oversee canteen facilities, restrooms, crèches, and worker statutory amenities."),
    ("Under the Factories Act, 1948, a 'Safety Officer' must be mandatorily appointed in every factory employing:", "1,000 or more workers (or where manufacturing processes carry severe toxic hazards)", ["250 or more workers", "100 or more workers", "50 or more workers"], "Section 40B mandates qualified safety officers to prevent industrial accidents, inspect machinery, and enforce occupational safety."),
    ("What does 'Human Resource Due Diligence' (HRDD) examine during corporate mergers and acquisitions (M&A)?", "Evaluating leadership talent, cultural compatibility, severance liabilities, pension obligations, and key retention risks", ["Auditing physical warehouse real estate boundaries and land deeds", "Inspecting the mechanical integrity of company transport trucks", "Recalculating past customer sales tax receipts"], "HRDD assesses post-merger integration risks, unrecorded labor liabilities, union friction, and talent flight that could derail merger synergies."),
    ("What is 'Decoupling' in institutional theory applied to corporate HR practices?", "Adopting formal HR policies (e.g., diversity charters) symbolically to gain legitimacy while daily operational practices remain unchanged", ["Separating the HR department into recruitment and payroll offices", "Terminating employment contracts of underperforming managers", "Splitting a conglomerate into two independent publicly traded firms"], "Decoupling highlights the gap between public ceremonial policy statements and actual entrenched managerial routines on the ground.")
]
